Build the real-time 3D "car stage" for a new React web app. Repo: /Users/atahankilic/Documents/ehl-munich-final, frontend at web/ (Vite 8 + React 19 + TypeScript, deps already installed: three 0.186, @react-three/fiber 9, @react-three/drei 10, motion, lucide-react). Path alias `@/` → web/src. Work ONLY inside web/src/three/ plus a dev harness entry (web/lab.html + web/src/lab/*) — other people are building the rest of web/src concurrently, don't touch their files (main.tsx, App.tsx, styles/, components/, screens/).

Product context: a BMW product manager's decision cockpit ("Successor"). On the Cars screen, today's car is shown in 3D; AI-recommended customer requirements for its successor are pinned to zones of the car (cockpit, rear seats, trunk, charging…). Clicking a zone flies the camera there. The visual world: near-black cabin glass, one BMW-like blue accent, refined (inspired by Fey's dark UI + a car's own HMI). Dark theme only. No BMW logos.

Design tokens to use (hard-code as constants in web/src/three/theme.ts; the app's CSS uses the same values): background #08090b, panel #0e1014, hairline #1d2027, text #e8ebf0, muted #8a93a3, blue #1c69d4, blue-bright #4d94ff, amber #f5a524, red #ff5a5f, green #3ccf91. Font: "Mona Sans Variable" (the app loads @fontsource-variable/mona-sans; in the lab, import it yourself).

Deliver `web/src/three/CarStage.tsx` exporting:
```ts
type ZoneDef = { label: string; pos: [number, number, number]; view: [number, number, number]; dist: number };
// pos/view are in a NORMALISED CAR FRAME: f = along length (-1 rear … +1 front), r = across (-1 left … +1 right, driver's view), h = up (0 ground … 1 roof). dist = camera distance in half-lengths.
type Hotspot = { zone: string; count: number; top: "Must" | "Should" | "Could"; aiOnly?: boolean };
type Orientation = { up: "+y" | "+z" | "-y" | "-z" | "+x" | "-x"; front: "+x" | "-x" | "+z" | "-z" | "+y" | "-y" };
interface CarStageProps {
  modelUrl: string | null;              // "/models/g60.glb" or "/models/g60/scene.gltf" or null → placeholder car
  orientation?: Orientation;            // how the model's own axes map; default {up:"+y", front:"+z"}
  zones: Record<string, ZoneDef>;
  hotspots: Hotspot[];
  selectedZone: string | null;
  onSelectZone: (zone: string | null) => void;
  hoverZone?: string | null;            // highlight from outside (list hover)
  mode?: "showroom" | "compact";        // compact = smaller thumbnail use (no controls, slow turntable)
  className?: string;
}
```
Requirements:
1. Load glTF/GLB with drei useGLTF (support both). Normalise: rotate by orientation so front = +X world, up = +Y; compute Box3; centre on origin with wheels on y=0; scale so length = 4.8 units. Derive the frame (half-length L, half-width W, height H) from the box so zones map to world: x = f·L, z = r·W (right = +Z when front = +X and up = +Y? derive correctly: with front +X, up +Y, the right-hand side is -Z or +Z — compute right = front × up and use it), y = h·H. Write a unit-testable pure function `zoneToWorld(zone, frame)` in web/src/three/frame.ts.
2. Placeholder when modelUrl is null or fails to load: a tasteful procedural car (rounded body + glasshouse + four wheels, satin dark-grey material) with the same normalised frame, plus a small overlay text "3D model not installed — showing a placeholder" (subtle, bottom-left). Must never crash the page (ErrorBoundary + Suspense with a quiet loading state: thin progress line + percentage from useProgress).
3. Studio look (the signature of the app — make it beautiful): dark studio, environment built from drei <Environment resolution={256}> with <Lightformer>s (long softbox strips above, rim lights, NO network HDRI downloads), a glossy dark floor (MeshReflectorMaterial, subtle, mirror ~0.4, blurred) fading into the background (fog or radial gradient), ContactShadows under the car, gentle bloom-free look (no postprocessing package; do not add deps). Car paint should look good on these specific models (they come with their own materials — boost envMapIntensity reasonably; don't replace their materials).
4. Camera: drei <CameraControls> with smooth fly-to (`setLookAt(..., true)`), constrained polar angle (don't go under the floor), min/max distance, no pan. Default "overview" pose = front-left three-quarter, slightly elevated (camera at f≈0.85, r≈-0.95 normalised, height ~0.45H, distance ~2.75L). Selecting a zone flies to target = zoneToWorld(pos), eye = target + normalised(view direction in world) × dist × L. Deselect → overview. Slow idle turntable (auto-rotate ~0.15 rad/s) only in compact mode or after 20 s idle in showroom mode; any user input stops it.
5. Hotspots: for each Hotspot, an anchored marker at the zone position via drei <Html> (center, zIndexRange set sensibly), rendered as a crisp DOM pin: 28px circle showing the count, Must = solid blue fill (#1c69d4) with a slow pulse ring, Should = blue outline on dark, Could = grey outline; aiOnly adds a dashed ring (AI suggestion not yet PM-confirmed). Label pill (zone label) appears on hover/selection. Pins behind the car body dim to ~30% opacity (use `occlude` raycast against the car meshes, or compute facing vs camera like dot(viewDir, camDir)). Selected pin scales up with a blue glow. Keyboard accessible: pins are buttons with aria-labels "Cockpit & displays — 4 recommendations".
6. Signature interaction — "zone focus": when a zone is selected, (a) camera flies there (~1.2 s, ease-out), (b) for interior zones (cockpit, front_seats, cabin, rear_seats) the car body turns x-ray: traverse meshes and lerp material opacity to ~0.12 with depthWrite false for exterior/body meshes (heuristic: all meshes; keep glass/interior if names hint at interior — keep it simple and robust: fade everything to 0.18 and draw a soft blue light pool / glow sprite at the zone), restore on exit; (c) a soft blue spot/glow at the zone position. Must be smooth (animate in useFrame, no React re-render per frame).
7. Performance: models are 200k–500k triangles. Use dpr={[1, 2]}, `frameloop="always"`, shadows off except ContactShadows (frames={1} after load), no per-frame allocations. Preload helper `preloadCar(url)`.
8. Accessibility & reduced motion: respect prefers-reduced-motion (no auto-rotate; camera moves instant).
9. Lab harness: web/lab.html + web/src/lab/main.tsx rendering CarStage full-screen on the dark background with a fake zone set (copy ZONES from src/bmw_req/car_zones.py: front, powertrain, assist, cockpit, front_seats, cabin, rear_seats, doors, chassis, charging, trunk — copy their pos/view/dist exactly) and fake hotspots, plus a small control strip to pick a zone, switch model URL (?model=/models/g60.glb query param) and orientation. Add `lab.html` to Vite build inputs only if needed for dev (dev server serves it automatically at /lab.html).
10. Verify visually. The dev server: `cd web && npx vite --port 5174 --strictPort` in background. Screenshot with headless Chrome (WebGL works with the system Chrome): `"/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome" --headless=new --disable-gpu-sandbox --use-angle=metal --enable-unsafe-swiftshader --window-size=1440,900 --virtual-time-budget=15000 --screenshot=/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-atahankilic-Documents-ehl-munich-final/c7f79221-413b-46d1-a535-ff2a11d3d1b2/scratchpad/lab-<name>.png "http://127.0.0.1:5174/lab.html?zone=cockpit"` (try flags until WebGL renders; if headless can't do WebGL, say so). Use the Read tool to look at the PNGs. Check: placeholder car renders, floor/lighting look premium, hotspots positioned on the car, zone focus x-ray works. If real model files exist in /Users/atahankilic/Documents/ehl-munich-final/data/models/ (another agent is downloading f70/g60/g70 right now — glb or gltf folders), test with them too by temporarily serving that folder: add a dev-only Vite middleware or simply `ln -s` is NOT allowed into web/public (don't copy confidential/large data into web/); instead configure the lab to fetch `/models/...` which the Vite config proxies to the API at 127.0.0.1:8600 (may not be running) — so for testing, run a tiny static server on 8600 yourself: `python3 -m http.server 8600 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory /Users/atahankilic/Documents/ehl-munich-final/data` (then /models/g60.glb resolves). Stop it when done. If models are present, CALIBRATE each model's orientation (which local axis is front/up) by rendering and looking, and report the correct Orientation per model (f70, g60, g70).
11. `cd web && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must pass for your files (ignore errors in files you don't own if others are mid-edit, but report them).
Report back: files, the public API, screenshots paths, calibrated orientations (if models were available), known limitations. Kill any background servers you started.

